### What is consumer lag?

Consumer lag is the most important health signal for a streaming pipeline. Each Kafka partition has a log-end offset (the newest message) and each consumer group commits the offset it has processed. Lag is the difference. At the National Bank of India, steadily growing lag means fraud alerts arrive minutes late.

```python
# Monitor consumer lag using kafka-consumer-groups CLI
# Run from the bank's monitoring server
```

```bash
# Check lag for the transaction-processor consumer group
kafka-consumer-groups.sh \
  --bootstrap-server kafka-broker:9092 \
  --describe \
  --group transaction-processor
```

```text
GROUP                  TOPIC              PARTITION  CURRENT-OFFSET  LOG-END-OFFSET  LAG
transaction-processor  bank-transactions   0          4500            5000            500
transaction-processor  bank-transactions   1          4800            5000            200
transaction-processor  bank-transactions   2          3200            5000            1800
```

Partition 2 has 1800 messages of lag, which needs investigation.

### Why does lag grow?

Lag grows when the consumer cannot keep up with the production rate. The common causes at a bank are slow per-message processing (complex fraud checks), too few consumers relative to partitions, or a slow downstream dependency (database write bottleneck).

```java
import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.KafkaConsumer;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.ConsumerRecords;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.ConsumerRecord;
import java.time.Duration;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

// Slow consumer: fraud check takes too long per record
KafkaConsumer<String, Transaction> consumer = new KafkaConsumer<>(props);
consumer.subscribe(List.of("bank-transactions"));

while (true) {
    ConsumerRecords<String, Transaction> records = consumer.poll(Duration.ofMillis(100));
    for (ConsumerRecord<String, Transaction> record : records) {
        Transaction txn = record.value();
        // This fraud check is slow and causes lag to grow
        FraudResult result = fraudService.deepCheck(txn);  // 500ms per record
        processResult(result);
    }
    consumer.commitSync();
}
```

### What is partition rebalancing?

Rebalancing happens whenever group membership changes: a consumer joins, leaves, or is considered dead after missing heartbeats. The group coordinator reassigns partitions so each live consumer owns a share.

```java
import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.ConsumerRebalanceListener;
import org.apache.kafka.common.TopicPartition;
import java.util.Collection;

public class BankRebalanceListener implements ConsumerRebalanceListener {
    @Override
    public void onPartitionsRevoked(Collection<TopicPartition> partitions) {
        // Commit offsets for partitions being taken away
        System.out.println("Revoking: " + partitions);
        consumer.commitSync();
    }

    @Override
    public void onPartitionsAssigned(Collection<TopicPartition> partitions) {
        System.out.println("Assigned: " + partitions);
        // Resume processing from committed offsets
    }
}

consumer.subscribe(List.of("bank-transactions"), new BankRebalanceListener());
```

During a naive (eager) rebalance, a stop-the-world pause occurs where no consumer processes messages. Cooperative incremental rebalancing reduces this by only moving the partitions that must move.

```java
// Enable cooperative rebalancing to minimize pause
props.put("partition.assignment.strategy",
    "org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.CooperativeStickyAssignor");
```

### How does back-pressure protect the pipeline?

Back-pressure is how a pipeline protects itself from overload. If a downstream stage like a database write is slower than ingestion, an unbounded buffer would grow until memory is exhausted. Back-pressure propagates the slowness upstream so the whole pipeline runs at the speed of its slowest stage rather than crashing.

```java
// In a pull-based consumer, back-pressure is natural:
// you simply poll less frequently when processing is slow
while (true) {
    ConsumerRecords<String, Transaction> records = consumer.poll(Duration.ofMillis(100));
    for (ConsumerRecord<String, Transaction> record : records) {
        // If this is slow, the next poll is delayed
        // which naturally reduces consumption rate
        bankDatabase.writeTransaction(record.value());
    }
    consumer.commitSync();
}

// Tune fetch sizes to control batch pressure
props.put("max.poll.records", "100");  // Smaller batches when processing is slow
props.put("fetch.max.bytes", "1048576");  // 1 MB max per fetch
```

### How do you scale consumers?

Within a consumer group, a partition is owned by exactly one consumer, so you cannot have more active consumers than partitions. Extra consumers sit idle. Partition count sets your maximum parallelism.

```text
Partitions: 6
Consumers:  3   --> Each consumer gets 2 partitions (good)
Consumers:  6   --> Each consumer gets 1 partition (max parallelism)
Consumers:  8   --> 6 active, 2 idle (wasted)
```

```bash
# Increase partitions to allow more consumers
kafka-topics.sh --alter \
  --topic bank-transactions \
  --partitions 12 \
  --bootstrap-server kafka-broker:9092
```

### How do you handle poison records with a dead-letter queue?

A poison record is a malformed message that keeps failing. You retry a bounded number of times and then route it to a dead-letter queue so one bad message never blocks its partition forever.

```java
import java.math.BigDecimal;

int MAX_RETRIES = 3;

for (ConsumerRecord<String, Transaction> record : records) {
    int attempts = 0;
    boolean success = false;
    while (attempts < MAX_RETRIES && !success) {
        try {
            processTransaction(record.value());
            success = true;
        } catch (Exception e) {
            attempts++;
            if (attempts >= MAX_RETRIES) {
                // Route to dead-letter queue
                dlqProducer.send(new ProducerRecord<>(
                    "bank-transactions-dlq",
                    record.key(),
                    record.value()
                ));
                System.err.println(
                    "Poison record sent to DLQ: " + record.key());
            }
        }
    }
}
```

### What monitoring should you set up?

Monitor lag, throughput, processing latency, and per-stage error rate. Alert when lag grows or errors spike.

```python
# Prometheus alerting rule for consumer lag
# bank_kafka_alerts.yml
alert_rules = """
groups:
  - name: kafka_consumer_health
    rules:
      - alert: HighConsumerLag
        expr: kafka_consumer_group_lag > 10000
        for: 5m
        labels:
          severity: critical
          team: bank-data-platform
        annotations:
          summary: "Consumer lag exceeds 10k for {{ $labels.group }}"

      - alert: DLQRateSpike
        expr: rate(kafka_dlq_messages_total[5m]) > 10
        for: 2m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "Dead-letter queue receiving >10 msgs/sec"
"""
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- Monitor the dead-letter queue itself: a rising DLQ rate signals an upstream data-quality problem, not just a bad record.
- Cooperative incremental rebalancing (CooperativeStickyAssignor) is the modern default: it reduces pause time from seconds to milliseconds.
- Idempotent processing is essential because a rebalance can cause reprocessing of in-flight messages.
- Partition count is a permanent decision: you can increase it but not decrease it without recreating the topic.
- Consider static group membership for long-lived consumers to avoid unnecessary rebalances on restart.

```java
// Static group membership to avoid rebalance on restart
props.put("group.instance.id", "bank-processor-instance-1");
// Consumer can restart within session.timeout without triggering rebalance
```
